Interventional & Active Recruiting Studies in the United States
Document generated from ClinicalTrials.gov live records

================================================================================
TRIAL ENTRIES (16 DISTINCT CLINICAL TRIALS)
================================================================================

`;

trials.forEach((t, idx) => {
  docText += `[TRIAL ${idx + 1}]
• Trial Name: ${t.title}
• Treatment Type: ${t.intervention}
• Trial Phase: ${t.phase}
• Recruiting Status: ${t.status}
• Locations: ${t.locationsFormatted || t.locations}
• Official ClinicalTrials.gov Link: ${t.url}

`;
});

docText += `================================================================================
TREATMENT APPROACHES & LOCATION SUMMARY
================================================================================

1. Treatment Approaches Observed Most Frequently:
• Targeted Therapies (Small Molecule Inhibitors): A major focus is on oncogene-specific mutations, particularly KRAS inhibitors targeting KRAS G12C (such as MK-1084 in trials NCT07286149 and NCT07252739) and KRAS G12D (TSN1611 in NCT06385925), as well as novel targeted agents like BMS-986488 and fingolimod (S1P receptor modulator).
• Immunotherapies and Checkpoint Inhibitor Combinations: Anti-PD-1 backbone agents (primarily Pembrolizumab / Keytruda and Nivolumab / Opdivo) are extensively used either in neoadjuvant resectable settings or in combination with novel investigational agents and cytokine/anti-inflammatory modulators (e.g., Certolizumab pegol).
• Antibody-Drug Conjugates (ADCs): Targeted antibody payloads targeting surface antigens on lung cancer cells, such as R-DXD (Raludotatug Deruxtecan) in squamous NSCLC, Ris-Rez (GSK5764227) in relapsed small cell lung cancer (SCLC), and Telisotuzumab Adizutecan targeting c-Met in advanced non-squamous NSCLC.
• Advanced Local, Inhaled, and Image-Guided Modalities: Innovative localized delivery systems including vascular-targeted photodynamic therapy/ablation (Padeliporfin VTP) via robotic-assisted bronchoscopy, aerosolized inhaled chemotherapy (Aerosol Gemcitabine), personalized accelerated chemoradiation (PACER), and targeted radioligand/radionuclide therapy (Lutetium Lu 177 Dotatate).
• Surgical Guidance and Molecular Imaging: Intraoperative near-infrared imaging agents (Abenacianine / VGT-309) to visualize tumor margins in real time during thoracic surgical resection.

2. Cities and Hospitals Appearing Most Frequently Across Locations:
• Prominent Hospitals & Cancer Centers:
  - The University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center (Houston, TX)
  - Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center (New York, NY and New Jersey regional network)
  - Stanford University School of Medicine / Stanford Cancer Center (Palo Alto & San Jose, CA)
  - Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine (Baltimore, MD)
  - MedStar Franklin Square Medical Center & Greenebaum Comprehensive Cancer Center (Baltimore, MD)
  - City of Hope National Medical Center & UCLA / Cedars-Sinai Medical Center (Los Angeles metropolitan area, CA)
  - University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus (Aurora, CO)
  - Mayo Clinic Hospital (Phoenix, AZ)
  - University of Illinois & University of Chicago (Chicago, IL)
  - Hollings Cancer Center at MUSC (Charleston, SC)
• Most Frequent Cities / Metropolitan Areas:
  - Houston, Texas
  - Baltimore, Maryland
  - New York, New York & Northern New Jersey (MSK network)
  - Greater Los Angeles Area (Los Angeles, Duarte, Beverly Hills, Santa Monica, CA)
  - San Francisco Bay Area / Silicon Valley (Palo Alto, San Jose, San Francisco, CA)
  - Chicago, Illinois
  - Central Florida (Orlando / Clermont / Tampa, FL)
`;

await write("tmp://document_content.txt", docText);
console.log("Document text prepared. Total length:", docText.length);
console.log("First 500 chars:\n", docText.slice(0, 500));

